Here's a simple **HTML + JavaScript** code snippet for a fake login page that captures user credentials. This example simulates the login process without a backend, but you can extend it to use a database or API:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Simple Login</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h2>Login</h2>
    <form id="loginForm">
        <label for="email">Email:</label>
        <input type="email" id="email" name="email" placeholder="Enter your email" required><br>

        <label for="password">Password:</label>
        <input type="password" id="password" name="password" placeholder="Enter your password" required><br>

        <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>

    <p id="loginMessage" style="color: blue; font-size: 18px; margin-top: 20px;"></p>

    <script>
        document.getElementById('loginForm').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
            event.preventDefault();

            const email = document.getElementById('email').value.trim();
            const password = document.getElementById('password').value.trim();

            // Simulate login (you can replace this with actual logic, e.g., database query)
            if (email === 'test@example.com' && password === '123456') {
                document.getElementById('loginMessage').textContent = 'Login successful!';
            } else {
                document.getElementById('loginMessage').textContent = 'Invalid credentials.';
            }
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How to Use:
- Replace the credentials (`test@example.com`, `123456`) with actual email and password.
- Save this code in a file (e.g., `login.html`) and open it in a browser to see the login form.
